{"content":{"agent":"general-purpose","blocks":[{"text":"Investigation complete. Findings:\n\n## Answer: YES — Codex writes a separate rollout file per sub-agent, and the trail to it resolves on disk.\n\n**Store layout** (`~/.codex/sessions/`): flat `YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-<ISO-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl`. No nesting, no distinct naming for children — 40 files here, all depth-3 date dirs.\n\n**Sub-agent rollouts are identified by `session_meta.payload.source`**: a *string* for top-level (`cli` 22, `exec` 5, `vscode` 2) but an *object* `{subagent: …}` for children (11 files). Two child shapes: `source.subagent.thread_spawn` (5 files; fields `parent_thread_id`, `depth`(=1), `agent_role`, `agent_path`, `agent_nickname`, plus top-level `forked_from_id`, `agent_path`, `agent_nickname`) and `source.subagent.other` (6 files). All 11 carry top-level `payload.parent_thread_id`.\n\n**Those files hold real turn-by-turn conversation**, not just activity: inside the 11 child rollouts — 678 `response_item/message`, 950 `reasoning`, 565 `custom_tool_call`(+outputs), 64 `agent_message`, 305 `turn_context`. They also contain 185 `sub_agent_activity` of their own, so nesting is recursive.\n\n**The trail:** `event_msg/sub_agent_activity` carries `{type, kind, event_id, occurred_at_ms, agent_thread_id, agent_path}`; `kind` ∈ started(15)/interacted(227)/interrupted(3). 6 distinct `agent_thread_id` values exist; **5 of 6 match a child rollout's `payload.id` on disk**, and all 11 child filenames embed their `payload.id`. So `agent_thread_id` → glob `**/rollout-*-<id>.jsonl` is a working resolver. (`inter_agent_communication_metadata.payload` carries only `trigger_turn` — no child pointer. `response_item/agent_message` carries `author`/`recipient`/`internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough.turn_id` — names, not thread ids.)\n\n## Latent bug in the current adapter\n\n`src/adapters/codex.ts:549-558` prefers `session_meta.payload.session_id` over the filename uuid. **On a sub-agent rollout, `session_id` is the parent's id** — for all 11 children `session_id != payload.id`, `session_id` is absent from the filename, and all 3 distinct child `session_id` values collide with an existing top-level rollout id (8 of 11 have `parent_thread_id == session_id`). Consequences if a child file ever reaches `captureCodexTranscript`: (a) child turns land in `codex:<parent>` with seqs numbered by the child's own line index, interleaving nonsense into the parent's `conversation.seq` (ids stay distinct — content is in `eventId`, schema.ts:252 — so no loss, but ordering is corrupt); (b) `readCursor/writeCursor` (codex.ts:560, 686) key on that same id, so parent and child share one cursor and thrash each other into full rescans.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n1. **Fix identity first (small, safe):** key codex sessions on `session_meta.payload.id` (equals the filename uuid for *all* 40 files, so top-level event ids are unchanged — no re-id churn), and when `source` is an object with `subagent`, emit `conversation: { id: \"codex:<id>\", parent: \"codex:<parent_thread_id>\" }`. This is exactly opencode's `parentID` → `conversation.parent` shape (`src/adapters/opencode.ts:407-418`) and claude-code's sidechain shape (`src/adapters/claude-code.ts:230-250`). Do this even before discovery — it removes a real corruption path.\n2. **Then add the trail walk** to meet the bar: on `event_msg/sub_agent_activity`, take `agent_thread_id`, glob `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*-<id>.jsonl`, recurse with a `visited` set (opencode's `childSessionIds`/recursion at opencode.ts:632-670 is the template; codex needs a filesystem glob instead of a CLI export). Expect misses — 1 of 6 ids had no file, presumably pruned.\n3. **Unknown I could not settle:** whether codex fires its hook with the *child's* `transcript_path`. cledger has no discovery walk today (`src/cli.ts:890,913` — hook stdin or explicit `--transcript` only), so if codex doesn't fire per-child hooks, step 2 is the *only* way children get captured. `codex --version` = 0.146.0; `codex --help` mentions no sub-agent/delegation subcommand, so this is undocumented surface — worth a note in the adapter comment either way.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"opencode-stable-seq","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a67735deb89658e859eb93287ce9a1491ec9d37f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-0c24f25cdd7ae98179123ddeeb0f28a95af6f598f4f84bed917dede5c90f3387","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.235"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-19T02:13:20.371Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"ac6f3540d95132cd3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Investigation complete. Findings:\n\n## Answer: YES — Codex writes a separate rollout file per sub-agent, and the trail to it resolves on disk.\n\n**Store layout** (`~/.codex/sessions/`): flat `YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-<ISO-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl`. No nesting, no distinct naming for children — 40 files here, all depth-3 date dirs.\n\n**Sub-agent rollouts are identified by `session_meta.payload.source`**: a *string* for top-level (`cli` 22, `exec` 5, `vscode` 2) but an *object* `{subagent: …}` for children (11 files). Two child shapes: `source.subagent.thread_spawn` (5 files; fields `parent_thread_id`, `depth`(=1), `agent_role`, `agent_path`, `agent_nickname`, plus top-level `forked_from_id`, `agent_path`, `agent_nickname`) and `source.subagent.other` (6 files). All 11 carry top-level `payload.parent_thread_id`.\n\n**Those files hold real turn-by-turn conversation**, not just activity: inside the 11 child rollouts — 678 `response_item/message`, 950 `reasoni