tability more than global order.\n\n## Evidence (file:line)\n\n- Running index assigned in `flattenParts`, `src/adapters/opencode.ts:291-302` (`let seq = 0; … seq++`), carried on `FlatPart` (`:164-169`) and read at `:585`.\n- Part id field is `OpencodePart.id` (`:122`); message id `OpencodeMessageInfo.id` (`:108`); session `OpencodeSessionInfo.id` (`:97`). All present in the export.\n- Cursor: `readCursor(repo, sessionId, \"parts\").count` — a **part count**, `:43`, `:567`; reset to 0 only when `cursor &gt; flat.length` (`:570`); written as `flat.length` (`:581`, `:616`).\n- No existing comment discusses part-id ordering; the only mutation comment is `:568-570`.\n- Test fixture ids are synthetic placeholders, not real format: `src/test/adapter-opencode.test.ts:7` `\"ses_test000000000000000000000\"`, `:28` `\"msg_user\"`, `:36` `\"prt_0\"` … `:85` `\"prt_7\"`.\n- `ConversationRef.seq` is `seq: number` (`src/schema.ts:153`) — **not validated as an integer** (`validateEvent`, `:282-293`), consumed only by `store.ts:611` (numeric sort) and `cli.ts:281` (display `#seq`). It *is* inside the identity subset via `conversation` (`schema.ts:260`) and hashed through `JSON.stringify` (`src/canonical.ts:9-10`), so any value must round-trip exactly — a 48-bit int does (&lt; 2^53). A string would also hash fine but would break `store.ts:611`'s subtraction and the type.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nAdopt the fix: `seq = parseInt(id.slice(4, 16), 16)` when `/^prt_[0-9a-f]{12}/` matches; keep the positional index as fallback for ids that don't match (dev/test ids exist in the wild, as shown). This is monotone in creation order, unique per session, deletion-proof, and needs no schema change. Downsides to accept: `cledger log` shows `#67580218395` instead of `#3` (consider rendering `seq` as an ordinal within the conversation rather than raw), and it's a **one-time identity break** — every existing opencode event re-captures under a new id, so gate it behind a raw-format bump (`opencode-export-json/2`) or accept one round of duplicates.\n\nDo the cursor at the same time: a part *count* still mis-resumes when a mid-session deletion is offset by new parts (`:570` only catches a net shrink). With sortable ids, store the last-captured part id and resume at `id &gt; cursor` — same monotone key, and it makes the \"unsettled tool holds the cursor back\" logic (`:602-604`) id-based too.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51955</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>14</tool_uses><duration_ms>158856</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","timestamp":"2026-08-19T02:12:50.914Z","type":"queued_command"},"injection_type":"attachment/queued_command"},"context":{"branch":"opencode-stable-seq","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a67735deb89658e859eb93287ce9a1491ec9d37f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-9e327915a44f9ccd5fd458a4d7cc4f22f2950ac1a88ecbfaeb71901fe42bac8e","kind":"context_injection","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.235"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-19T02:12:50.914Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"attachment":{"commandMode":"task-notification","prompt":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa509a04dda91edeb</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01PMBuBc5MCywtAcKsvSkX4V</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-evintunador-repos-evintunador-conversation-ledger/224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d/tasks/aa509a04dda91edeb.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Investigate opencode part id ordering\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Answer\n\n**Yes — opencode part ids are reliably orderable, and a stable integer `seq` falls straight o