{"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01MVvouartGcDBwvmVF21rvs","input":{"command":"git push -q -u origin reads-default-scoped 2>&1|tail -5; gh pr create --title \"0.23.0: reads scope to the current branch, and a closed reader can say ENOTCONN\" --body \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nRoadmap item **\"Reads default to unscoped\"**, plus a real bug in the stdout guard that shipped in #6.\n\n## Reads default to scoped\n\n`readEvents` applied reachability only when the caller asked, and `cledger export` never asked — so a consumer reading the ledger got every branch's conversations, including work abandoned and never merged. Storage has been per-commit all along and push has been branch-scoped since 0.14.0; reads were the last place \"the ledger\" still meant everything this machine ever captured.\n\n`ReadOptions.reachableFrom` now defaults to `HEAD`. The fix sits in `readEvents` rather than in `export` precisely because the consumers that matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go through the CLI at all. `export` gains `--all` alongside `--rev`, matching `log`.\n\n**The opt-out is an explicit `null`, not \"unset.\"** `undefined` means \"the caller did not say\" and resolves to `HEAD`. That distinction is the point: the callers that genuinely want every local event are few and deliberate, while the ones that silently got everything by not asking were the bug. Five now say so at their call site:\n\n| Caller | Why it opts out |\n|---|---|\n| `cledger scan`, `cledger review` | a secret does not stop being one because its branch is not checked out |\n| `cledger inspect` | an event id is a global handle |\n| `renormalize` | otherwise it strands preserved lines on every branch but the current one |\n| `cledger show <id>` | you named one conversation, so reachability is not the question you asked |\n\n**Consequence worth knowing:** after `cledger sync --fetch`, conversations anchored to commits you have not fetched are invisible by default — correct, since you cannot reach them, but it can look like nothing arrived. `--all` answers \"did it arrive\"; the default answers \"does it belong to this history.\"\n\nTwo transport tests had been quietly conflating those two questions — they build repos with *deliberately unrelated* history, so the fetched events genuinely are unreachable. They now assert both facts separately rather than one by accident.\n\nMeasured on this repo's own ledger: `export` 11,138 events vs `export --all` 11,816 — 678 events from branches this history does not reach were previously dumped unconditionally.\n\n## `ENOTCONN` is also a closed reader\n\nFollow-up to #6's stdout guard, which matched `EPIPE` alone. On macOS Node does not always back stdio with a pipe, and writing to a socket-backed stdout whose peer has closed reports **`ENOTCONN`** instead — so `cledger log --json | head -1` still exited 1 with `write ENOTCONN`. The stack trace was gone; the failure was not.\n\nIt only reproduced under enough concurrent load to change how far the teardown had progressed, which is why #6's suite passed. `ECONNRESET` is matched too, for the half-closed case. Which errno arrives is an accident of what kind of file descriptor Node handed the process, never of what the user did.\n\nThe test now asserts stderr is **empty** rather than free of one named errno — the narrower assertion passed while the command was still broken, which is exactly the failure mode worth designing against.\n\n## Testing\n\n`npm test`: **227 tests, 227 pass, 0 fail** — and stable across 3 consecutive full runs, which is how the load-dependent `ENOTCONN` case was confirmed fixed rather than hidden.\n\nNew `src/test/read-scope.test.ts` builds a repo where `side` branches off `main` and is never merged, then pins: the default scope, the `null` opt-out, an explicit rev, that a narrowing filter (`kind`) does not silently widen the branch scope, `export` vs `export --all` vs `--rev` through the real binary, and that `show` still finds a conversation captured on another branch.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013a944mewfSjMmV598jZzFb\nEOF\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -3"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"opencode-stable-seq","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a67735deb89658e859eb93287ce9a1491ec9d37f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-f1525d5db043c0b5f78c2cace4489db0f5ebc3ef04e1cdbdf1a8758a9c60b1bd","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.235"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-19T02:17:31.017Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"reads-default-scoped","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01MVvouartGcDBwvmVF21rvs","input":{"command":"git push -q -u origin reads-default-scoped 2>&1|tail -5; gh pr create --title \"0.23.0: reads scope to the current branch, and a closed reader can say ENOTCONN\" --body \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nRoadmap item **\"Reads default to unscoped\"**, plus a real bug in the stdout guard that shipped in #6.\n\n## Reads default to scoped\n\n`readEvents` applied reachability only when the caller asked, and `cledger export` never asked — so a consumer reading the ledger got every branch's conversations, including work abandoned and never merged. Storage has been per-commit all along and push has been branch-scoped since 0.14.0; reads were the last place \"the ledger\" still meant everything this machine ever captured.\n\n`ReadOptions.reachableFrom` now defaults to `HEAD`. The fix sits in `readEvents` rather than in `export` precisely because the consumers that matter — intent-reca