{"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01U68fxdM4FHNGu1Fjzd8L4h","input":{"command":"git add -A && git status --short && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\n0.25.0: opencode seq comes from the part id, and log prints a position\n\nConfirms the open question — opencode's part ids *are* reliably orderable —\nand takes the fix it unblocks, plus the cursor and display work that go with\nit.\n\n**The ids.** opencode mints `prt_` + 12 hex + 14 random base62, where the hex\nis a 48-bit `Date.now() * 4096 + per-millisecond counter`: its own Identifier\nscheme, ULID-shaped without being a ULID. Verified against a live 1.18.10\nstore: 859 parts, zero ordering violations within any session.\n\n**The fix.** `conversation.seq` now comes from the part's own id, making it a\nproperty of the part rather than of its neighbours. opencode's store is\nmutable — `session revert`, `message.removed`, `part.removed` delete parts out\nof the middle — and under a positional index every part after the hole shifted\ndown, re-capturing under new ids because `seq` is part of event identity.\nDuplicates in the ledger, permanently, for content that never changed.\n\nThe scheme is chosen per *session*, never per part. Ids opencode did not mint\nexist in the wild, and a session mixing a 48-bit timestamp with a small integer\nwould sort into nonsense. Unless every part parses and the values are distinct,\nthe whole session keeps positional numbering and behaves exactly as before.\n\n**The cursor moves to the same key.** It stored a part count, which only\nnotices parts going away when the session gets *shorter* — so a deletion offset\nby a new part left the count looking healthy while pointing at the wrong place.\nIt is now a high-water mark over `seq`, which has nothing to be offset by. The\nunsettled-tool rule is unchanged in effect: it holds below the held part's seq\ninstead of its index.\n\n**The display.** A 48-bit seq is not a counter, and printing it raw turned\nevery opencode row into `opencode:9f2a#67580218395`. `cledger log`'s human\nformat now prints a position within the conversation, ranked over the events\nthe read returned so that hiding the harness's bookkeeping does not renumber\nthe turns around it — positions stay stable under `--kind`, `--with-state` and\nfriends, which is why they can have gaps. Ties share a rank, since a reasoning\nblob and the turn it belongs to are written at the same seq on purpose.\n`--json` and `export` are untouched: a machine reading the ledger wants the\nreal ordering key, not a per-view position.\n\nForward-only. Already-captured opencode events keep their positional seqs and\nsort correctly alongside derived ones; only a from-scratch re-capture would\nfile second copies, the same one-round cost every identity change here carries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013a944mewfSjMmV598jZzFb\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -1"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"opencode-stable-seq","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a67735deb89658e859eb93287ce9a1491ec9d37f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-bc36ea3befb9d4382242aacf3ebee08edf919c6208dd0647c25f219cfa57f993","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.235"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-19T02:36:04.109Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"opencode-stable-seq","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01U68fxdM4FHNGu1Fjzd8L4h","input":{"command":"git add -A && git status --short && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\n0.25.0: opencode seq comes from