{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Investigate an open design question for the conversation-ledger project at /Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger.\n\nBACKGROUND: cledger's opencode adapter assigns each captured event a `conversation.seq` from a running positional index over the parts it reads. opencode stores sessions in a MUTABLE SQLite store where `session revert`, `message.removed` and `part.removed` can delete parts out of the middle of a session. Deleting a part shifts the positions of everything after it, and since `seq` is part of event identity, the shifted parts re-capture under NEW ids — duplicates in the ledger. The proposed real fix is to derive `seq` from opencode's own part ids instead of a running index, which REQUIRES those ids to be reliably orderable. The roadmap says this is \"worth confirming before committing to it.\"\n\nQUESTION: Are opencode's part ids reliably orderable (monotonic / sortable in creation order)? And if so, can a stable integer `seq` be derived from them?\n\nDo these things:\n\n1. Read /Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/src/adapters/opencode.ts closely. Report with file:line refs:\n   - Exactly where and how `seq` is currently assigned (the running index).\n   - What id fields opencode's export JSON actually gives per message and per part (field names).\n   - How the cursor works and what it's keyed on.\n   - Any existing comments about part ids or ordering.\n\n2. Read the opencode tests at src/test/adapter-opencode.test.ts and any fixtures under src/test/fixtures/ that show the real shape of an `opencode export` payload. Report the ACTUAL FORMAT of message ids and part ids (e.g. are they `prt_01H...` ULIDs? UUIDs? incrementing integers? nanoids?). Quote only the ID STRINGS themselves and their field names — nothing else from the fixtures.\n\n3. Determine whether opencode is installed on this machine (`opencode --version`, `which opencode`). If it is, and ONLY using non-destructive read-only commands, find out the real id format — e.g. `opencode session list` output structure. Do NOT run `opencode export` on real user sessions and do NOT print any conversation content.\n\n4. Research the id scheme: if the ids look like ULIDs or a known sortable scheme (ULID, KSUID, snowflake, xid, cuid2), say which, and state clearly whether that scheme guarantees lexicographic sort == creation order. If they are UUIDv4 or nanoid, say plainly that they are NOT orderable and the proposed fix cannot work as stated.\n\n5. Given the answer, assess the proposed fix and give a recommendation. Consider alternatives if ids are not orderable — e.g. keying `seq` on a hash of the part id, using the part id as an opaque string key instead of an integer, or leaving `seq` positional and deduping differently. Note any constraint from cledger's schema (read src/schema.ts `eventId` and the `conversation` field type) — in particular whether `seq` MUST be an integer.\n\nCRITICAL SAFETY CONSTRAINT: opencode sessions are real conversation transcripts that may contain secrets and private content. Do NOT print, quote, or echo any message text, prompt, tool output, or `content`/`text` field values from any real session or fixture. ID strings and field names only. If a step needs content to answer, skip it and say so.\n\nWrite any scratch scripts to $CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp. Do not modify any repo files.\n\nDeliver a compact report: direct answer to the QUESTION, the evidence with file:line refs, whether `seq` must be an integer, and a concrete recommendation. Max ~30 lines.","type":"text"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"opencode-stable-seq","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a67735deb89658e859eb93287ce9a1491ec9d37f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-351de7da49ae22355f283df8fe95b5184bdd83b5d2482251b169e60f7c2e0bd4","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a48